 -7.855892181396484e-05, 0.0030364990234375, -0.0108795166015625, -0.01195526123046875, 0.0200958251953125, -0.0120849609375, 0.0251007080078125, -0.0072021484375, -0.0099334716796875, -0.00830078125, -0.00888824462890625, -0.0203094482421875, 0.007587432861328125, -0.0047454833984375, 0.02838134765625, 0.0121917724609375, -0.022796630859375, 0.0047149658203125, 0.01456451416015625, -0.0245361328125, 0.009613037109375, 0.00803375244140625, 0.009613037109375, 0.0161895751953125, -0.00473785400390625, 0.052459716796875, -0.01104736328125, 0.005084991455078125, 0.01131439208984375, 0.02752685546875, -0.0211181640625, -0.0029163360595703125, 0.025787353515625, -0.043853759765625, -0.0027294158935546875, -0.0118408203125, -0.018890380859375, -0.002376556396484375, 0.00884246826171875, 0.0096893310546875, -0.04736328125, 0.020233154296875, -0.0156402587890625, 0.0018548965454101562, -0.02239990234375, 0.00547027587890625, -0.0090789794921875, 0.003627777099609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "We investigate how the precise temporal order of excitatory and inhibitory synaptic inputs affects firing in sparse threshold-reset neural networks, particularly in the fast-synapse limit where synaptic delays vanish. While it is common to analyze such systems using aggregate synaptic measures, we show that the microscopic sequence of input arrivals can have a persistent macroscopic impact, even when standard convergence criteria are met.\n\nComponentwise weak convergence of signed synaptic kernels\u2014meaning that, for each neuron, the excitatory and inhibitory synaptic measures converge weakly (in distribution) to a limiting measure\u2014does not, by itself, determine the fast-synapse limit of a sparse threshold-reset network. Here, \"weak convergence\" refers to convergence of integrals against continuous test functions, and the \"Dirac delta at zero\" ($\\delta_0$) is the measure concentrated at zero delay. In these networks, the exact temporal order in which excitatory and inhibitory inputs arrive at a neuron can crucially affect whether the neuron fires, even if the total synaptic input converges in distribution.\n\nTo illustrate this, we work within a causal event protocol with clamped refractoriness and smooth positive-delay kernels (each synaptic event has a strictly positive delay and the kernel is smooth). We construct two families of networks whose excitatory and inhibitory measures both converge weakly to $\\delta_0$, but with reversed microscopic arrival orders of excitation and inhibition. The distinction is as follows: in the \"excitatory-first\" family, excitation arrives just before inhibition, while in the \"inhibitory-first\" family, inhibition arrives first.\n\nThis arrival order is critical. In the excitatory-first family, a target neuron fires, while in the inhibitory-first family, it does not, precisely when the initial state $x$ and pulse masses $a$ (excitatory) and $b$ (inhibitory) satisfy $x+a-b<\\theta\\le x+a$, where $\\theta$ is the firing threshold. The \"strict margin\" condition\u2014meaning $x+a-\\theta>0$\u2014ensures that the neuron's post-excitation state exceeds threshold by a positive amount. This margin guarantees robustness: the firing outcome persists under small perturbations to the initial state, aggregate E/I pulse masses, or bounded drift in the system, because such changes cannot bridge the gap created by the margin.\n\nWe now extend this microscopic effect to the network level. Consider a moderately sparse random block graph that is Dale-compatible (i.e., each neuron is either purely excitatory or purely inhibitory, in accordance with Dale's law), with block sizes $q_N$ satisfying $q_N\\to\\infty$ and $q_N/N\\to0$ as the network size $N$ grows. Both network families share the same graph structure and initial data. Along any deterministic joint scaling $\\varepsilon_N\\downarrow0$, the difference in their population-averaged firing counts is $1/2+o_{L^1}(1)$, meaning that, on average, half the population exhibits a different firing response in the two systems, up to a vanishing error. (Here, $o_{L^1}(1)$ denotes a sequence of random variables converging to zero in $L^1$ norm.) This quantifies the macroscopic impact of arrival order, showing it is not washed out by averaging.\n\nA bounded-degree construction, together with a later probe (i.e., a subsequent input or test pulse delivered after the initial firing event), confirms that this discrepancy is macroscopic and can persist through the reset mechanism of the neurons. The probe demonstrates that the difference in firing outcomes is not erased by the reset: neurons that fired in one family but not the other continue to respond differently to subsequent inputs.\n\nHowever, when synaptic kernels have fixed positive delays and only finitely many classes, the network can enter a stable regime. Before the firing threshold is grazed, typewise-mixing (i.e., sufficiently random mixing between neuron types) sparse networks converge to a delayed class mean-field system, where the effect of arrival order is averaged out. For directed Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi graphs with mean degree $\u03bb_N$ and empirical type distribution $\u03c0_N$, the deviation from the mean-field limit is bounded by $O_P(\u03bb_N^{-1/2}+\\|\u03c0_N-\u03c0\\|_1)$, where $O_P(\\cdot)$ denotes stochastic boundedness in probability, $\u03bb_N\\to\\infty$, $\u03bb_N/N\\to0$, and $\u03c0$ is the limiting type distribution. This result separates the regime of stable averaging at fixed delay from the singular collapse regime, where the delay vanishes and arrival order information is lost.\n\nIn summary, our findings show that componentwise weak convergence of synaptic measures is insufficient to determine the fast-synapse limit in threshold-reset networks: the microscopic order of excitatory and inhibitory arrivals encodes information essential for the firing response, and this information can persist at the macroscopic level in sparse networks. This highlights the importance of considering arrival order in modeling and analyzing such systems, especially in the singular limit of vanishing delays. Limitations of this work include the focus on specific network architectures (e.g., block graphs and Dale-compatible structures) and event protocols. Future directions include investigating whether similar arrival-order effects arise in networks with more general connectivity patterns, under different dynamical rules (such as stochastic or continuous-time resets), or in the presence of synaptic plasticity. Open questions also remain regarding the impact of correlated input timings and the role of network heterogeneity in modulating these effects."}}